# Routh II.Arts.196-198 — Sylvester's and Poinsot's measures of the time
# — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "The Advanced Part of a Treatise on the Dynamics of a System of
# Rigid Bodies", Part II (Macmillan, 6th ed. 1905), Art. 196: "Sylvester's
# measure of the time. When a body turns about a fixed point its motion in
# space is represented by making its momental ellipsoid roll on a fixed plane.
# This gives NO REPRESENTATION OF THE TIME occupied by the body in passing from
# [one position to another]."
#
# THE GAP POINSOT LEAVES. Art. 143's construction is complete as geometry and
# silent as chronology. It tells you the path the instantaneous axis takes --
# the polhode rolling on the herpolhode -- but nothing about how fast. Rolling
# is a purely kinematic statement; you can traverse the same polhode at any
# schedule and the picture is unchanged.
#
# SYLVESTER'S FIX. Take a second body CORRELATED with the first -- their
# ellipsoids of gyration confocal, so that
#
#     I'^-1 = I^-1 - k*Identity        (Arts. 192-195)
#
# and give it the same angular momentum. Its motion differs from the first
# body's by
#
#     w - w'  =  k * L
#
# a rotation about the invariable line at the CONSTANT rate k*G. Art. 196 puts
# it as a rotation "G t (1/A - 1/A') about the invariable line": the second
# ellipsoid turns uniformly relative to the first. So the angle between the two
# bodies is proportional to t, and reading it off is reading a clock. Art. 198
# records Poinsot's variant, a cone rolling on a plane which "turns uniformly
# round the invariable line" -- the same uniform rotation supplying the same
# missing quantity.
#
# WHY THIS IS THE ONLY UNIFORM THING IN SIGHT. In a torque-free asymmetric
# motion almost nothing advances at a constant rate: not |w|, not the polhode
# angle, not the precession. G and T are constant but they are numbers, not
# clocks -- they do not advance at all. The correlated body's relative rotation
# is a quantity that grows linearly with t, and it exists only because of the
# confocal relation.
#
# WHAT IS MEASURED HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT. The clock RATE k*G is exact at the
# instant of correspondence, and is verified below:
#
#     |w - w'| = k*G = 1.283854061020956   at t = 0, to machine precision
#
# Sustaining that uniform rotation requires the two bodies to be held in the
# correlated ATTITUDES of Art. 195. Start them merely alike, as here, and their
# attitudes diverge -- because different inertias turn differently -- and the
# difference ceases to be k*L: measured, |w - w'| drifts to 1.4574566464474867
# by t = 3 and 1.4319086423736707 by t = 7, and its direction leaves the
# invariable line. The cells print that drift rather than hiding it.
#
# So this notebook establishes the rate and the mechanism, and shows precisely
# what Art. 195's attitude condition is doing: without it there is no clock.
# The two bodies' own |w| are printed alongside, to make the contrast plain --
# neither of them is uniform either.
#
# SETUP NOTE. Body 2's inertia matches no simple shape and is supplied directly
# with inertia_tensor; posim keeps a user-supplied tensor rather than
# recomputing it. g_constant = 0 so the two bodies do not attract each other.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: two boxes with identical angular momentum tumbling at
# different rates. The angle between them is the clock -- the one thing in the
# whole motion that advances uniformly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/nsh/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p2_sylvester_time
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_sylvester_time.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_sylvester_time.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], position = [-5, 0, 0] }
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], position = [ 5, 0, 0], inertia_tensor = [[10, 0, 0], [0, 7.391304347826088, 0], [0, 0, 1.4285714285714284]] }
set obj0.angular_momentum = [1.5, 12.75, 0.125]
set obj1.angular_momentum = [1.5, 12.75, 0.125]

# the clock rate k*G, and its direction -- along the invariable line
norm(obj0.angular_velocity - obj1.angular_velocity)
normalize(obj0.angular_velocity - obj1.angular_velocity)
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)

# neither body's own angular velocity is uniform ...
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)
run 3 steps 3
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)
# ... and neither is the difference, once the attitudes have diverged
norm(obj0.angular_velocity - obj1.angular_velocity)
normalize(obj0.angular_velocity - obj1.angular_velocity)
# the angle the clock WOULD accumulate by t = 3 at the correspondence rate
1.2838540610209558 * 3

run 4 steps 4
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)
norm(obj0.angular_velocity - obj1.angular_velocity)
normalize(obj0.angular_velocity - obj1.angular_velocity)
# and by t = 7 -- valid only while the correlated attitudes are held
1.2838540610209558 * 7
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], position = [-5, 0, 0] }
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], position = [ 5, 0, 0], inertia_tensor = [[10, 0, 0], [0, 7.391304347826088, 0], [0, 0, 1.4285714285714284]] }
set obj0.angular_momentum = [1.5, 12.75, 0.125]
set obj1.angular_momentum = [1.5, 12.75, 0.125]

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.03
